"texte": [
"FAMILY ENTERPRISE GOVERNANCE ADVISORY REPORT — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR FAMILY BUSINESS CONTINUITY (ZURICH - MILAN - PARIS). To: The Family Council and Supervisory Board of 'Vandenberghe-Keller Industrial Group'. Subject: Designing an institutional succession framework for the transition from second-generation sibling partnership to third-generation cousin consortium. Family-owned enterprises represent the backbone of European and global industrial capitalism, frequently outperforming widely held public corporations over multi-decade horizons thanks to patient capital allocation, deep sector stewardship, and strong employee loyalty. Yet empirical research confirms the enduring validity of the international proverb 'shirtsleeves to shirtsleeves in three generations': fewer than 30 % of family businesses survive the transition to the second generation, and barely 12 % remain viable under family ownership into the third.",
"The root cause of this generational attrition lies rarely in market disruption alone, but in the structural entanglement of three distinct institutional spheres identified by Harvard researchers Renato Tagiuri and John Davis in their 'Three-Circle Model': the family system (governed by emotional bonds, egalitarian affection, and lineage loyalty), the ownership system (governed by capital return, dividend policy, and equity liquidity), and the business management system (governed by meritocratic competence, operational accountability, and competitive market discipline).",
"Crisis erupts most acutely during executive succession when founding or second-generation patriarchs conflate family birthright with executive competence. Appointing an eldest child to the chief executive role out of primogeniture tradition—or dividing operational divisions equally among siblings regardless of strategic coherence—alienates talented non-family executives, triggers destructive sibling rivalries in the boardroom, and deprives the enterprise of the specialised leadership required to navigate technological and international expansion.",
"Conversely, an abrupt, poorly governed transition toward external professional management carries its own institutional hazards. When a family board recruits a high-profile external chief executive without establishing clear boundaries between family shareholder governance and operational authority, the incoming leader frequently clashes with family members accustomed to informal intervention in daily operations, resulting in rapid executive turnover and strategic paralysis.",
"As a family enterprise expands into a third-generation 'cousin consortium' comprising dozens of geographically dispersed shareholders—many of whom no longer work inside the business—a further existential threat emerges: the divergence between 'active operational family managers', who favour reinvesting profits into long-term industrial growth, and 'passive non-executive family shareholders', who demand high annual dividend payouts or seek to liquidate their equity stakes at steep valuations.",
"Safeguarding institutional continuity across generations requires formalising governance structures well before a health crisis or sudden retirement forces hasty improvisation. The cornerstone of this architecture is a legally binding 'Family Constitution' (or Family Charter) negotiated transparently across branches, which establishes strict meritocratic prerequisites for family members seeking executive roles—such as external university credentials, at least five years of verified career progression outside the family firm, and competitive assessment by independent executive search consultants.",
"Simultaneously, separating emotional family dynamics from corporate oversight demands creating a dual-track governance structure: a 'Family Council' dedicated to nurturing shared values, next-generation education, and philanthropic initiatives, alongside a professionalised 'Board of Directors' featuring a majority of genuinely independent non-executive directors empowered to select, evaluate, and if necessary replace the chief executive—whether family or external—strictly on objective performance criteria.",
"Ultimately, enduring family enterprises endure not because they resist institutionalisation in the name of familial informality, but because they transform personal dynastic legacy into an objective governance discipline capable of uniting family stewardship with world-class executive meritocracy."
