    "texte": [
      "CORPORATE FINANCE ADVISORY PAPER — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE OF APPLIED FINANCIAL ECONOMICS (LONDON - FRANKFURT). In modern corporate finance, financial engineering—defined as the design, structuring, and deployment of customised securities, derivative instruments, and multi-tiered capital architectures—occupies an ambivalent analytical position. Proponents celebrate it as an indispensable engine of market completeness that lowers financing costs and allocates risk to those best equipped to bear it, whereas critics contend that much sophisticated structuring merely redistributes existing economic wealth from taxpayers, creditors, and employees toward equity sponsors and financial intermediaries.",
      "Under the classical Modigliani-Miller irrelevance propositions, in frictionless capital markets devoid of taxes, bankruptcy costs, or information asymmetries, the market value of an enterprise is determined exclusively by the earning power of its real operating assets rather than by how its cash flows are sliced among debt and equity holders. In the real world, however, institutional frictions are pervasive, creating legitimate avenues through which financial engineering genuinely expands aggregate enterprise value.",
      "Genuine value creation occurs most visibly when structured financing resolves acute information asymmetries or completes missing risk markets. Consider an infrastructure developer constructing an offshore wind farm: by separating predictable post-construction operational cash flows from high-risk early construction phases through project-specific securitisation and interest-rate swaps, financial engineers enable pension funds to supply low-cost, long-duration capital that would otherwise remain unavailable, thereby turning an unbankable industrial project into a viable reality.",
      "Conversely, a substantial portion of contemporary financial engineering operates not by expanding the economic pie, but by engineering asymmetric wealth transfers across stakeholders. Leveraged recapitalisations—wherein a private equity sponsor causes an acquired corporation to issue high-yield debt solely to fund an immediate special dividend—transfer balance-sheet solvency risk onto existing unsecured bondholders and employees while locking in risk-free cash returns for equity holders.",
