"texte": [
"MACRO-FINANCIAL AND INDUSTRIAL POLICY ESSAY — EUROPEAN REVIEW OF POLITICAL ECONOMY AND INDUSTRIAL STRATEGY. Over the past four decades, advanced capitalist economies have undergone a profound structural transformation termed 'corporate financialisation': the increasing dominance of financial motives, financial markets, and financial engineering metrics over traditional industrial production and long-term capital formation within non-financial corporations.",
"Historically, during the post-war industrial expansion, non-financial corporations operated predominantly under a 'retain-and-reinvest' allocation regime: operating profits were retained inside the firm and ploughed back into physical plant modernisation, proprietary research laboratories, and workforce skill accumulation. Under financialised governance, however, this model gave way to a 'downsize-and-distribute' paradigm prioritising immediate shareholder payouts and balance-sheet asset-lightness.",
"Empirical macroeconomic data across North America and Western Europe reveal a striking divergence between corporate profitability and real productive investment. While corporate net profit margins reached multi-decade highs, aggregate net fixed capital formation as a share of corporate cash flow stagnated, as a growing fraction of operating surpluses was diverted into open-market share repurchases, elevated dividend distributions, and speculative financial asset acquisitions.",
"In extreme cases, major industrial conglomerates effectively transformed their internal corporate treasuries into quasi-hedge funds—borrowing cheaply in commercial paper markets to invest in high-yielding structured credit securities, sovereign debt arbitrage, and minority equity stakes rather than funding core manufacturing innovation. While lucrative during benign monetary cycles, this financialisation of corporate balance sheets exposed industrial enterprises to severe liquidity contagion during financial crises.",
"Simultaneously, the pursuit of 'asset-light' financial metrics—designed to inflate Return on Invested Capital (ROIC) by removing physical factories and specialized equipment from the balance sheet—led Western manufacturers to outsource critical production stages and engineering capabilities to offshore contract manufacturers. Over time, hollowing out domestic manufacturing ecosystems severed the vital feedback loop between factory-floor process engineering and next-generation product innovation, eroding national industrial resilience.",
"Labor markets and income distribution have similarly borne the brunt of financialised corporate strategies. To sustain high quarterly free-cash-flow payouts amid sluggish top-line demand, firms relied heavily on domestic wage compression, fissured subcontracting, and underinvestment in apprenticeship pipelines, contributing to stagnant labour productivity growth and widening inequality between capital owners and wage earners.",
