"texte": [
"ALTERNATIVE ASSET MANAGEMENT & PRIVATE CREDIT MARKET ANALYSIS — EUROPEAN CAPITAL MARKETS RESEARCH INSTITUTE (LONDON - ZURICH). Over the past fifteen years, global corporate finance has experienced a profound structural disintermediation: the steady migration of mid-market and leveraged corporate lending away from regulated commercial banks toward alternative investment funds—principally private credit funds, direct lending vehicles, distressed debt specialists, and multi-strategy asset managers.",
"This historic shift originated in the post-2008 regulatory overhaul of the banking sector. Under stringent Basel III capital adequacy rules, leverage ratios, and supervisory stress tests, commercial banks faced sharply higher capital charges when holding non-investment-grade or illiquid corporate loans on their balance sheets. Retreating from leveraged buyout financing and complex mid-market lending, banks left a vast funding vacuum that private credit funds—which exceed 1.7 trillion US dollars in global assets under management—rapidly filled.",
"For corporate borrowers and private equity sponsors, borrowing directly from alternative investment funds offers compelling operational advantages over traditional syndicated bank loans or public high-yield bonds. Private credit managers provide certainty of execution without syndication market-flex risk, tailor bespoke 'unitranche' facilities combining senior and subordinated debt into a single blended-rate instrument, and execute multi-hundred-million-dollar commitments within days rather than months.",
"Moreover, because private credit funds raise long-term locked-up capital from institutional investors—such as sovereign wealth funds, pension plans, and university endowments—rather than relying on short-term retail bank deposits, they are insulated from classic bank-run liquidity mismatches. During episodes of acute public bond market freezes, direct lenders have repeatedly stepped in to finance major corporate acquisitions when traditional investment banks were unable to underwrite syndicated debt.",
"Nevertheless, the rapid migration of corporate credit into opaque private markets raises significant micro- and macro-prudential concerns among central banks and financial stability regulators. First, private credit loans carry substantially higher floating interest margins—frequently 300 to 500 basis points above benchmark money-market rates—combined with upfront origination fees and strict call-protection penalties that burden borrowing companies during prolonged high-rate cycles.",
"Second, financial stability supervisors warn of 'opacity, valuation lag, and hidden leverage interconnections'. Unlike publicly traded corporate bonds whose prices adjust daily to reflect deteriorating borrower health, private credit loans are marked to model quarterly by fund managers themselves, potentially masking rising borrower distress through consensual payment-in-kind amendments ('amend-and-extend' practices) until defaults cluster abruptly.",
