"CORPORATE DEBT SUSTAINABILITY & LIABILITY MANAGEMENT MEMORANDUM — GLOBAL RESTRUCTURING & CAPITAL STRUCTURE PRACTICE (LONDON - NEW YORK). To: The Audit and Finance Committee of 'Trans-European Manufacturing & Materials Group'. Subject: Assessing corporate debt sustainability, refinancing maturity walls, and interest-coverage resilience amid monetary policy normalisation. Following more than a decade of near-zero policy interest rates and abundant central bank liquidity that encouraged non-financial corporations worldwide to accumulate record levels of debt—exceeding 90 trillion US dollars globally—the abrupt return of elevated benchmark interest rates and volatile credit spreads has restored debt sustainability to the forefront of executive risk management.",
"The transmission of tighter financial conditions onto corporate balance sheets operates through two distinct channels: immediate floating-rate repricing and delayed fixed-rate 'refinancing maturity walls'. Enterprises carrying substantial unhedged floating-rate bank loans experience an instantaneous surge in quarterly cash interest expense as money-market reference rates (SOFR, EURIBOR) reset upward. Conversely, corporations that locked in ultra-low fixed-rate bonds between 2019 and 2021 enjoy a temporary grace period, only to confront a severe 'coupon shock'—frequently seeing refinancing rates jump from 2 % to over 6.5 %—when those bonds mature.",
