    "texte": [
      "CORPORATE STRATEGY AND COMPETITIVE DYNAMICS ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR STRATEGIC MANAGEMENT (FONTAINEBLEAU - LONDON). For nearly four decades, mainstream strategic management rested upon Michael Porter's foundational paradigm of 'sustainable competitive advantage': the belief that an enterprise could construct durable structural barriers—through scale economies, proprietary patents, distribution exclusivity, or high switching costs—capable of protecting above-average economic rents for decades. Today, however, in industries driven by continuous digital, algorithmic, and scientific innovation, static competitive moats are eroding at unprecedented velocity.",
      "As Columbia Business School strategist Rita Gunther McGrath has argued, modern hyper-competitive markets operate under a regime of 'transient advantage'. Because cloud infrastructure, open-source artificial intelligence models, and modular global contract manufacturing have drastically lowered the capital barriers required to launch rival products, any successful innovation triggers instantaneous competitive swarming—compressing the profitable exploitation window of a new offering from ten years to barely eighteen or twenty-four months.",
      "Paradoxically, the very organisational routines that enable an incumbent enterprise to exploit an existing competitive advantage efficiently become the primary source of its vulnerability during technological transitions. Having optimised its engineering workflows, sales incentives, and capital budgeting around defending a lucrative legacy product line, the market leader suffers from 'competence traps and structural inertia', mistaking temporary financial profitability for permanent market invulnerability.",
      "This erosion is particularly visible in software, enterprise services, and consumer electronics, where functional feature differentiation evaporates within a single product release cycle. Once a pioneering firm introduces a breakthrough capability—such as conversational workflow automation or predictive diagnostics—rivals rapidly replicate equivalent functionality through agile engineering sprints or third-party API integrations, transforming what was once a premium differentiator into a baseline commodity expectation.",
      "Furthermore, traditional intellectual property protection—long regarded as an unassailable legal fortress—frequently proves porous in fast-moving innovation ecosystems. Competitors routinely engineer around narrow patent claims, exploit jurisdictional enforcement delays, or bypass proprietary hardware architectures entirely by shifting value capture toward open-standard software ecosystems and data-driven network effects.",
      "To thrive in an environment where every competitive advantage is inherently temporary, enterprises must replace static moat defence with 'dynamic capability orchestration' (the Teece framework). Rather than treating strategy as a five-year defensive plan, agile market leaders manage a continuous portfolio of strategic waves—simultaneously harvesting mature cash-cow offerings, scaling emerging growth platforms, and funding exploratory internal ventures designed to cannibalise legacy products before external disruptors do so.",
