"texte": [
"INDUSTRIAL STRATEGY AND VALUE-CHAIN ECONOMICS ANALYSIS — EUROPEAN CENTRE FOR GLOBAL VALUE CHAINS AND INDUSTRIAL POLICY. In contemporary global industries, competitive supremacy rarely stems from outperforming rivals across every single stage of production. Instead, the most profitable enterprises achieve structural dominance by identifying and monopolising 'strategic bottlenecks' (chokepoints) within complex, multi-tiered global value chains—capturing disproportionate economic rents while forcing upstream suppliers and downstream customers into asymmetric dependency.",
"Economic theorists describe this architecture as 'bottleneck value capture' (championed by strategists Michael Jacobides and Carliss Baldwin). Within any modular industrial ecosystem—whether semiconductor manufacturing, electric-vehicle batteries, commercial aviation, or cloud computing—the vast majority of components become commoditised over time, leaving one or two technically indispensable nodes characterised by extreme intellectual property density, massive capital barriers, and high switching costs.",
"The global semiconductor industry exemplifies this bottleneck dominance with crystalline clarity. While dozens of firms compete fiercely in smartphone assembly or standard memory chips, extreme ultraviolet (EUV) lithography machines—without which no advanced sub-3-nanometre processor can be fabricated—are produced by a single European equipment manufacturer orchestrating a tightly controlled network of optical and laser sub-suppliers, enabling it to command extraordinary margins and dictate industry-wide technological roadmaps.",
"Beyond physical machinery, dominant firms consolidate value-chain control through 'proprietary architectural standards and ecosystem lock-in'. By establishing the dominant software operating system, industrial automation protocol, or AI developer framework—and enforcing exclusive licensing terms alongside closed application programming interfaces (APIs)—an orchestrator firm transforms independent complementors into dependent ecosystem participants while extracting recurring tollbooth fees on every transaction.",
"Equally potent is 'upstream resource and refining control' in green-transition supply chains. Dominant industrial powers have demonstrated that owning raw mining concessions matters far less than controlling the midstream chemical refining and precursor-material processing stages. By cornering over 80 % of global refining capacity for battery-grade lithium, cobalt, and rare-earth permanent magnets, midstream bottleneck operators exercise decisive pricing leverage and geopolitical influence over downstream Western automakers and turbine manufacturers.",
"For enterprises trapped in non-bottleneck segments of a dominated value chain, the strategic consequences are severe: squeezed between powerful upstream component monopolists and concentrated downstream retail or platform gatekeepers, mid-tier manufacturers suffer persistent margin compression and reduced capacity to self-finance R&D.",
"Escaping value-chain subordination requires deliberate 'bottleneck-breaking and ecosystem-reshaping strategies'. Dependent firms can sponsor open-source technical standards to commoditise a rival's proprietary bottleneck, form pre-competitive R&D consortia to co-finance alternative midstream refining or tooling capacity, or pursue targeted vertical integration into critical sub-components where intellectual property differentiation can be rebuilt.",
"Ultimately, competitive strategy in an interconnected industrial world is a contest over architectural control. Enterprises that merely execute efficiently within value chains designed and governed by others remain perpetually vulnerable to margin extraction; lasting market leadership belongs to those that architect the critical standards, technologies, and bottlenecks around which entire industries must orbit."
