"texte": [
"EXECUTIVE FORESIGHT AND BUSINESS-MODEL TRANSFORMATION BRIEFING — EUROPEAN CENTRE FOR CORPORATE RENEWAL AND INNOVATION (ZURICH - LONDON). Across industrial history, market-leading enterprises rarely collapse because their leaders are incompetent or unaware of emerging technologies; rather, they succumb to 'business-model orthodoxy'—an inability to reimagine how the firm creates, delivers, and captures value when technological, regulatory, or demographic shifts invalidate historical industry assumptions.",
"Empirical studies of corporate longevity demonstrate that incumbents routinely misinterpret early signals of industry disruption through 'dominant-logic filtering'. When pioneering start-ups introduce radically simplified business models—such as subscription software replacing perpetual licences, direct-to-consumer digital banking replacing physical branch networks, or modular electric platforms replacing internal combustion powertrains—incumbent executives initially dismiss them as low-margin niche curiosities unsuited to their demanding flagship customers.",
"Compounding this perceptual blind spot is 'resource-allocation asymmetry' within mature corporations. Annual capital budgeting processes systematically favour incremental improvements to existing cash-cow business lines—which offer predictable 20 % operating margins and immediate payback—while starving nascent, unfamiliar business models whose early unit economics appear messy, cannibalistic, and uncertain.",
"When disruption accelerates past the tipping point, panicked incumbents frequently commit the error of 'cosmetic digital grafting': bolting a digital mobile interface or an artificial-intelligence chatbot onto an unchanged legacy cost structure and bureaucratic distribution model, thereby incurring higher IT overhead without altering the underlying economics of value delivery.",
"Anticipating structural disruption before financial performance deteriorates requires institutionalising 'systematic periphery scanning and weak-signal detection'. Rather than benchmarking exclusively against traditional industry peers in annual strategy reviews, forward-looking boards track cross-industry analogies, monitor venture-capital funding clusters in adjacent domains, and study the behaviour of 'non-consumers'—potential buyers currently excluded from the market by high prices or product complexity.",
"Once a credible threat to the core business model is identified, corporate renewal depends upon adopting Charles O'Reilly and Michael Tushman's 'ambidextrous organisation architecture'. Under this dual-track model, the enterprise continues to optimise its legacy core business for operational efficiency while launching an autonomous 'new-venture business unit' endowed with ring-fenced multi-year funding, distinct performance metrics (such as user adoption and learning velocity rather than immediate EBITDA), and direct reporting access to the chief executive.",
"Crucially, successful business-model reinvention requires leaders to challenge their own sacred industry dogmas through 'assumption-reversal workshops'—explicitly asking how an unconstrained outsider would design the business from scratch if every conventional rule regarding pricing, asset ownership, or distribution channels were inverted.",
"Ultimately, challenging an established business model while it is still profitable demands rare executive courage. Enterprises that endure across technological revolutions are those willing to cannibalise their own legacy profit engines voluntarily rather than waiting for external disruptors to render them obsolete."
