      "Empirical research in corporate finance, however, decisively refutes this executive bias. Longitudinal studies demonstrate that diversified conglomerates routinely trade at a 'conglomerate valuation discount' of 10 % to 15 % relative to the sum of their standalone parts, primarily because corporate headquarters misallocate cross-subsidised capital from high-return growth units toward underperforming legacy divisions. Conversely, enterprises that practise disciplined, proactive portfolio pruning consistently generate superior total shareholder returns over ten-year horizons.",
