"INTANGIBLE CAPITAL & INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY STRATEGY REPORT — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR THE KNOWLEDGE ECONOMY (LONDON - ZURICH). Over the past half-century, the asset composition of leading global corporations has undergone a profound structural inversion: whereas physical plant, machinery, and inventory accounted for over 80 % of S&P 500 enterprise market value in 1975, intangible assets—comprising proprietary software, algorithmic models, patents, clinical data, brand equity, and organisational know-how—now represent nearly 90 % of corporate market valuation.",
"As economists Jonathan Haskel and Stian Westlake have demonstrated in their seminal work on 'capitalism without capital', intangible assets exhibit four distinctive economic properties—scalability, sunkenness, spillovers, and synergies—that fundamentally alter competitive dynamics. Unlike physical factories, which face capacity limits and wear out through use, digital algorithms, chemical formulas, and brand architectures can be replicated across global markets at near-zero marginal cost while actually appreciating in value through network adoption.",
"Yet despite their decisive contribution to enterprise value, intangible assets remain notoriously under-managed and poorly measured by traditional accounting frameworks. Under prevailing International Financial Reporting Standards (IFRS) and US GAAP, internally generated R&D, software training datasets, customer relationships, and brand-building expenditures are expensed immediately as operating costs rather than capitalised on the balance sheet—creating a blind spot where boards rigorously audit physical warehouse inventories while lacking basic visibility into their most valuable intellectual capital.",
