"CORPORATE DIPLOMACY & GLOBAL GOVERNMENT AFFAIRS REPORT — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE OF GEOECONOMICS AND INTERNATIONAL BUSINESS (BRUSSELS - LONDON - SINGAPORE). In an era defined by resurgent industrial policy, sovereign infrastructure tenders, and strategic trade rivalries, multinational corporations can no longer treat diplomacy as the exclusive preserve of foreign ministries. Modern 'economic diplomacy'—operating as a two-way interplay between state commercial statecraft and corporate non-market strategy—has become decisive in securing market access, resolving regulatory deadlocks, and protecting cross-border investments.",
"At the state-to-corporate level, national governments actively deploy diplomatic leverage to advance the commercial bids of domestic flagship enterprises in strategic sectors such as aerospace, high-speed rail, telecommunications, nuclear energy, and defence. High-level ministerial trade missions, bilateral investment treaties (BITs), and concessional export-credit financing packages backed by state export-credit agencies (ECAs) frequently tip the competitive balance in multi-billion-dollar sovereign procurement tenders where technical specifications alone are closely matched.",
"Simultaneously, major multinational enterprises have institutionalised their own internal 'corporate diplomacy functions' led by former senior diplomats, trade negotiators, and regulatory strategists. Rather than relying solely on traditional domestic lobbying, corporate diplomats engage directly with foreign ministries, multilateral development banks, international standard-setting bodies, and civil society coalitions to shape regulatory frameworks upstream before restrictive legislation is drafted.",
