"MULTINATIONAL FX STRATEGY & CAPITAL ALLOCATION MEMORANDUM — GLOBAL TREASURY & CORPORATE STRATEGY OFFICE, 'EURO-LUXE & PRECISION INSTRUMENTATION SA'. To: The Executive Committee. Subject: Managing structural exchange-rate volatility across commercial pricing, supply-chain footprint, and cross-border capital repatriation. For corporate groups generating more than 70 % of their revenues outside their home currency zone, prolonged exchange-rate swings—such as a 15 % to 25 % appreciation of the euro or Swiss franc against the US dollar, Japanese yen, or emerging-market currencies—pose strategic dilemmas that cannot be resolved through short-term financial derivatives alone.",
"The primary commercial trade-off confronts export-oriented groups with an acute dilemma between 'protecting unit profit margins' and 'defending international market share'. When a European manufacturer's home currency appreciates sharply against the US dollar or yen, maintaining constant home-currency profit margins requires raising local retail prices abroad—a strategy known as 'complete exchange-rate pass-through'—which risks pricing the firm's products out of competitive foreign markets.",
"Conversely, absorbing the currency shock internally by holding foreign local prices unchanged—a strategy termed 'pricing-to-market'—preserves foreign customer volume and shelf space, but directly compresses consolidated operating margins when foreign sales proceeds are converted back into the stronger home reporting currency.",
"In emerging markets experiencing chronic currency depreciation and high domestic inflation—such as Turkey, Argentina, Nigeria, or Egypt—multinational subsidiaries face a compounding 'trapped-cash and dividend repatriation dilemma'. Even when local operating units achieve strong nominal revenue growth, stringent central bank foreign-exchange controls and wide gaps between official and parallel exchange rates can prevent subsidiaries from converting local cash surpluses into hard currency, exposing accumulated balances to relentless real purchasing-power erosion.",
