"texte": [
"GLOBAL MACROECONOMICS & TRADE STRATEGY ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR INTERNATIONAL ECONOMIC RELATIONS (BRUSSELS - GENEVA). For decades, orthodox international macroeconomics viewed persistent bilateral trade surpluses and deficits largely as benign reflections of comparative advantage and intertemporal saving-investment choices across nations. Today, however, massive structural trade imbalances—manifested in chronic manufacturing surpluses in East Asia paired with persistent current-account deficits in major Western consumer economies—have become primary catalysts of geopolitical friction, industrial hollowing-out, and supply-chain vulnerability.",
"Macroeconomists point out that persistent current-account imbalances stem fundamentally from structural domestic saving-investment asymmetries rather than tariffs alone. Economies characterised by high household saving rates, subdued domestic consumption, and state-directed industrial credit subsidies channel vast excess savings into export-oriented manufacturing capacity—generating structural overcapacity in steel, solar panels, electric vehicles, and batteries that spills onto global markets at depressed prices.",
"Conversely, economies with low domestic saving rates, expansive fiscal deficits, and open capital accounts—most notably the United States and several European economies—absorb those global surpluses by running sustained merchandise trade deficits, financed by capital inflows into domestic government bonds, real estate, and equity markets. While this arrangement provided Western consumers with decades of cheap imported goods and low inflation, it progressively eroded domestic manufacturing employment and strategic industrial ecosystems.",
"The geopolitical consequences of asymmetric trade dependencies become acutely visible during supply disruptions or diplomatic crises. When an importing economy relies on a single surplus nation for over 70 % of its active pharmaceutical ingredients, solar wafers, or permanent magnets, commercial interdependence mutates into coercive 'weaponised interdependence'—enabling the dominant supplier to threaten export restrictions as geopolitical leverage.",
"In response to structural overcapacity and asymmetric dependencies, deficit economies have abandoned passive free-trade orthodoxy in favour of 'defensive trade remedies and industrial rebalancing'. Countervailing anti-subsidy duties, carbon border adjustment mechanisms (CBAM), and local-content procurement rules aim to offset state-subsidised export pricing and rebuild domestic manufacturing capacity in strategic sectors.",
"Yet unilateral tariff escalation carries severe macroeconomic hazards for globally integrated enterprises. Import tariffs on intermediate industrial inputs—such as steel, aluminium, and electronic components—raise production costs for domestic downstream manufacturers, provoke retaliatory tariffs against high-value Western agricultural and aerospace exports, and reroute trade flows indirectly through third-party transshipment hubs without eliminating underlying global saving-investment imbalances.",
"Sustainable rebalancing of global trade dependencies therefore requires coordinated macroeconomic and corporate adaptation. At the policy level, surplus economies must stimulate domestic household consumption and social safety nets while deficit nations boost domestic savings and productivity investment; at the corporate level, multinational firms must build multi-regional 'in-region-for-region' production footprints capable of operating profitably inside each major trade bloc regardless of tariff walls.",
"Ultimately, persistent global trade imbalances are neither self-correcting market curiosities nor purely bilateral disputes. Reshaping global economic dependencies into a stable, reciprocal order requires combining targeted safeguards against state-subsidised overcapacity with domestic macroeconomic reforms and resilient, regionally balanced corporate supply chains."
