    "texte": [
      "INTERNATIONAL TRADE POLICY & GEOECONOMIC STRATEGY ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR INTERNATIONAL TRADE AND GLOBAL GOVERNANCE (GENEVA - BRUSSELS). For nearly four decades following the conclusion of the Uruguay Round and the creation of the World Trade Organization (WTO) in 1995, global economic policy was anchored in an unquestioned liberal consensus: that progressive tariff elimination, deep cross-border supply-chain integration, and rules-based multilateral dispute settlement would simultaneously maximise global efficiency and foster geopolitical stability.",
      "Today, that post-Cold War free-trade paradigm is undergoing profound structural erosion. Confronted with the weaponisation of critical mineral supply chains, pandemic-era medical shortages, and fierce technological rivalry between major powers, governments across North America, Europe, and Asia have subordinated pure Ricardian comparative advantage to national security imperatives, supply-chain resilience, and strategic industrial autonomy.",
