"texte": [
"SOVEREIGN DEBT & INTERNATIONAL POLITICAL ECONOMY BRIEFING — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR PUBLIC FINANCE AND MONETARY GOVERNANCE (LONDON - BRUSSELS). In an era of hyper-mobile global capital, deeply integrated sovereign bond markets, and real-time algorithmic currency trading, the classical Westphalian notion of unfettered national economic sovereignty confronts formidable market constraints. Whenever elected governments announce unfunded fiscal expansions, unorthodox monetary interventions, or abrupt regulatory overhauls, international financial markets exercise an instantaneous, ruthless referendum through sovereign bond yields and exchange rates.",
"Political economists describe this disciplinary mechanism as the return of the 'bond vigilantes'—institutional fixed-income investors, sovereign wealth funds, and macro hedge funds that sell off a nation's government bonds whenever fiscal deficits or inflationary policies threaten real debt sustainability. As demonstrated starkly during the 2022 United Kingdom gilt-market crisis and recurring emerging-market currency routs, a sudden spike in sovereign borrowing yields can force a government to reverse flagship fiscal policies within days to avert pension-fund insolvency and currency collapse.",
"Macroeconomist Robert Mundell's classic 'open-economy trilemma' (the impossible trinity) illustrates the structural constraint binding sovereign policymakers: a nation cannot simultaneously maintain free cross-border capital mobility, a fixed or managed exchange rate, and an independent domestic monetary policy tailored to local employment conditions. Economies seeking unrestricted access to global capital pools inevitably surrender a degree of autonomous macroeconomic control.",
