"texte": [
"DIGITAL COMPETITION & PLATFORM ECONOMICS ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR COMPETITION POLICY AND DIGITAL MARKETS (BRUSSELS - LONDON). Over the past two decades, digital multi-sided platforms—spanning search engines, mobile operating systems, e-commerce marketplaces, social networks, and cloud infrastructure—have fundamentally reshaped global commerce. By connecting millions of consumers and third-party merchants instantaneously, platform ecosystems have lowered search costs and unlocked immense consumer convenience; yet their distinctive economic mechanics have simultaneously generated unprecedented market concentration.",
"Unlike traditional industrial firms constrained by rising marginal production costs, digital platforms benefit from powerful 'direct and indirect network effects' paired with near-zero marginal distribution costs. As more buyers join an e-commerce marketplace, more sellers are attracted to the platform, which in turn draws even more buyers—creating a self-reinforcing flywheel that rapidly tips markets toward a single dominant 'winner-take-all' or 'winner-take-most' gatekeeper.",
"Once a platform achieves entrenched gatekeeper status, it occupies an extraordinary dual role: acting simultaneously as the 'neutral infrastructure referee' governing marketplace rules and as a 'direct commercial competitor' selling its own private-label products or proprietary apps on that very same platform. Antitrust authorities across Europe and North America have documented systematic abuses arising from this structural conflict of interest.",
"Specifically, dominant gatekeepers frequently engage in 'algorithmic self-preferencing, data appropriation, and ecosystem tying'. By ranking their own retail products or services at the top of search results, harvesting non-public sales telemetry generated by independent third-party merchants to launch rival private-label clones, and bundling default apps into mobile operating systems, gatekeepers entrench monopoly rents while stifling challenger innovation.",
"For years, traditional competition enforcement struggled to curb platform dominance because classic antitrust doctrine relied heavily on 'consumer-welfare price tests'—asking whether a merger or business practice raised immediate retail prices for consumers. Because digital platforms frequently offer nominally 'zero-price' services monetised through targeted advertising or merchant commissions, conventional price-centric antitrust models failed to capture harms to innovation, privacy, and merchant choice.",
"To overcome the slowness of retrospective antitrust litigation, jurisdictions have enacted proactive 'ex-ante digital competition regimes', epitomised by the European Union's Digital Markets Act (DMA) and the United Kingdom's Digital Markets, Competition and Consumers Act. Rather than waiting years to prove monopoly abuse in court, ex-ante rules designate major platforms exceeding quantitative user and turnover thresholds as regulated 'gatekeepers' bound by bright-line behavioural obligations.",
"These statutory obligations mandate 'data portability, third-party app-store sideloading, messaging interoperability, and bans on self-preferencing'—requiring gatekeepers to allow business users to steer customers toward cheaper off-platform payment channels and prohibiting the use of non-public merchant data to compete against platform sellers.",
"Ultimately, regulating digital platform ecosystems requires walking a delicate economic tightrope: curbing anti-competitive gatekeeper lock-in and restoring contestable market entry without dismantling the genuine network efficiencies and seamless user integration that make digital platforms valuable to consumers and small businesses alike."
