"texte": [
"BEHAVIOURAL STRATEGY & DECISION-GOVERNANCE ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR BEHAVIOURAL ECONOMICS AND EXECUTIVE JUDGMENT (FONTAINEBLEAU - LONDON). Classical management theory models senior corporate executives as hyper-rational economic agents who evaluate strategic alternatives objectively by weighing marginal probabilities and net present values. Yet decades of empirical research in behavioural economics—pioneered by Daniel Kahneman, Amos Tversky, and Richard Thaler—demonstrate that even the most experienced chief executives and investment committees are systematically swayed by predictable cognitive biases and group dynamics.",
"One of the most costly cognitive distortions in corporate capital allocation is 'escalation of commitment and the sunk-cost fallacy'. When a flagship product launch, overseas market entry, or enterprise IT transformation runs hundreds of millions of euros over budget and falls behind schedule, rational financial logic dictates evaluating only future incremental cash flows. Instead, executives frequently pour additional capital into failing projects to avoid admitting initial misjudgment and protecting personal reputational prestige.",
"Equally pervasive is 'executive overconfidence and the planning fallacy'. Empirical audits of major infrastructure and M&A initiatives reveal that leadership teams consistently underestimate project completion timelines and integration costs while overestimating their ability to outperform competitors—a bias amplified by corporate promotion systems that reward bold, charismatic certainty while penalising nuanced probabilistic caution.",
"In boardrooms and executive committees, individual cognitive distortions are frequently magnified by 'groupthink and authority-induced conformity', famously analysed by psychologist Irving Janis. When a dominant chief executive reveals their preferred strategic option early in a meeting, junior executives and independent directors unconsciously self-censor dissenting data, interpret ambiguous market signals as supporting the leader's thesis (confirmation bias), and converge prematurely on flawed consensus.",
"Furthermore, strategic resource allocation suffers from 'availability heuristics and recency bias'. Leadership teams disproportionately overweight vivid, recent market events—such as a sudden quarterly demand spike or a headline-grabbing competitor announcement—while underweighting slower, structural demographic or technological shifts that lack immediate dramatic visibility.",
"Because cognitive biases operate unconsciously, simply exhorting executives to 'think more objectively' proves wholly ineffective. Behavioural decision theorists emphasize that overcoming systematic judgment errors requires redesigning 'institutional decision architecture and choice hygiene' inside the boardroom.",
"Leading organisations implement four structural debiasing mechanisms: conducting mandatory 'pre-mortem exercises' (Gary Klein's technique asking teams to imagine the project has failed catastrophically three years hence and work backward to diagnose causes); appointing a formal 'Devil's Advocate' with protected dissent status on every major capital proposal; requiring anonymous written voting prior to open board discussion so senior voices do not anchor junior opinions; and establishing automatic 'stage-gate kill criteria' before capital is committed.",
"Ultimately, superior strategic judgment in complex enterprises does not stem from eliminating human intuition or replacing executives with rigid algorithms. Rather, it emerges from surrounding experienced executive intuition with disciplined institutional guardrails that surface dissenting evidence, counteract overconfidence, and separate personal ego from capital allocation."
