"texte": [
"SUSTAINABLE CAPITAL MARKETS & BLENDED FINANCE REPORT — EUROPEAN CENTRE FOR DEVELOPMENT AND TRANSITION FINANCE (LONDON - LUXEMBOURG - NAIROBI). Closing the multi-trillion-dollar global financing gap required to achieve the United Nations Sustainable Development Goals (SDGs) and Paris Agreement climate targets demands mobilising private institutional capital at unprecedented scale. Because sovereign fiscal budgets and traditional bilateral aid cover only a fraction of required green-grid, clean-water, and climate-adaptation investments, financial innovation has coalesced around three catalytic instruments: labelled green and social bonds, verifiable impact investing, and blended finance structures.",
"Green bonds—fixed-income securities whose proceeds are ring-fenced exclusively to finance eligible environmental projects such as utility-scale solar farms, electrified rail corridors, or energy-efficient buildings—have expanded into a multi-trillion-dollar global asset class. By accessing dedicated green institutional mandates, credible issuers frequently capture a modest borrowing-cost discount termed the 'greenium' while diversifying their fixed-income investor base.",
