    "texte": [
      "STRATEGIC INNOVATION & BUSINESS-MODEL TRANSFORMATION ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR INDUSTRIAL DYNAMICS AND COMPETITIVE STRATEGY. In popular business discourse, the term 'disruption' is routinely misused as a synonym for any incremental product upgrade or aggressive marketing campaign. In rigorous strategic theory—pioneered by Harvard economist Clayton Christensen—'disruptive innovation' describes a specific, counter-intuitive competitive process whereby a resource-constrained challenger successfully topples entrenched market leaders by redefining the underlying architecture of value creation, delivery, and pricing.",
      "Unlike 'sustaining innovations'—which improve product performance along dimensions already valued by an incumbent's most profitable mainstream customers—disruptive innovations initially perform worse on traditional metrics. Entering via either 'low-end footholds' (serving price-sensitive customers overserved by bloated, expensive legacy offerings) or 'new-market footholds' (converting non-consumers through radically simplified, accessible solutions), disruptors trade raw technical sophistication for convenience, modularity, and dramatically lower unit prices.",
      "Incumbent market leaders rarely fail out of technological blindness; rather, they are paralysed by 'asymmetric motivation and value-network lock-in'. Because their cost structures, sales commissions, and quarterly profit targets are calibrated to serve high-margin enterprise clients, rational incumbent executives deliberately cede the low-margin bottom tier to upstarts—viewing the retreat upward toward premium segments as sound financial discipline.",
      "Yet as underlying enabling technologies—such as cloud computing, modular battery chemistry, or generative AI inference—improve along a steeper trajectory than customer demand can absorb, the challenger's initially basic offering rapidly reaches performance parity with mainstream requirements while retaining its superior cost structure and digital agility. By the time incumbents recognise the existential threat, the disruptor has captured the volume core of the market and starved legacy players of reinvestment cash flow.",
