"texte": [
"DIGITAL ECONOMICS & MARKET STRUCTURE REPORT — EUROPEAN CENTRE FOR DIGITAL COMPETITION AND PLATFORM GOVERNANCE (BRUSSELS - LONDON). Over the past two decades, the architectural organisation of global commerce has shifted decisively from linear industrial value chains toward multi-sided digital platforms. Rather than manufacturing physical goods inventory internally, platform orchestrators—spanning e-commerce marketplaces, mobile app ecosystems, cloud infrastructure hyperscalers, and ride-hailing networks—create digital infrastructure that matches external producers and consumers at global scale.",
"While early digital theorists predicted that the internet would democratise commerce and foster frictionless atomistic competition, empirical market outcomes reveal an extraordinary 'winner-take-most oligopolistic concentration'. Across search, mobile operating systems, digital advertising, and cloud infrastructure, two or three dominant hyperscale ecosystems routinely capture over 75 % to 85 % of global market share and industry profit pools.",
"This extreme concentration is driven by four mutually reinforcing economic mechanisms: 'cross-side network effects' (where every new merchant attracts additional buyers, increasing platform utility exponentially), 'zero marginal distribution costs', 'data-driven algorithmic learning loops' that continuously improve search relevance and ad targeting with scale, and 'high ecosystem switching costs' engineered through bundled subscriptions, proprietary cloud APIs, and non-portable user histories.",
"Once a platform achieves entrenched gatekeeper dominance, it exercises unprecedented 'private regulatory power' over millions of dependent merchants, software developers, and content publishers. Unilaterally setting commission take-rates as high as 15 % to 30 %, altering ranking algorithms overnight without explanation, and controlling customer payment interfaces, dominant platforms capture the lion's share of ecosystem value while shifting inventory and labour risks onto fragmented third-party sellers.",
"Furthermore, dominant digital platforms sustain their supremacy through 'envelopment strategies and pre-emptive killer acquisitions'. By leveraging massive free cash flows from a core monopoly—such as search advertising or enterprise software—to subsidise adjacent emerging services at zero marginal price, or by acquiring nascent startups before they mature into credible platform challengers, incumbents neutralise competitive threats upstream.",
"For traditional brick-and-mortar manufacturers and retailers, platform concentration creates an acute 'intermediation dilemma'. Listing products on dominant marketplaces provides immediate access to hundreds of millions of global shoppers, yet simultaneously surrenders direct customer relationships, pricing autonomy, and granular consumer behavioural data to an intermediary that may eventually launch competing private-label alternatives.",
"Confronted with entrenched platform concentration, regulators have introduced 'ex-ante interoperability, data-portability, and anti-self-preferencing mandates' (such as the European Union Digital Markets Act), while forward-looking merchants pursue 'multi-homing and direct-to-consumer (D2C) sovereignty'—diversifying across multiple sales channels, cultivating owned first-party customer communities, and leveraging open-protocol commerce standards.",
"Ultimately, the platform economy has unlocked remarkable transactional efficiency and global market reach, yet unchecked concentration threatens long-term innovation and commercial pluralism. Sustaining a dynamic digital economy requires pairing pro-competitive interoperability regulation with disciplined merchant multi-homing strategies."
],
