"texte": [
"INTERNATIONAL REGULATORY & MARKET-ACCESS BRIEFING — GLOBAL TRADE POLICY & COMPLIANCE PRACTICE (BRUSSELS - GENEVA - WASHINGTON). Over the past half-century, successive rounds of multilateral and regional trade negotiations have reduced average ad valorem import tariffs across major industrial economies to historically low levels below 3 % to 4 %. Yet international exporters increasingly report that actual market access has grown more restrictive rather than freer, impeded by a dense, rapidly expanding web of 'non-tariff barriers' (NTBs).",
"Unlike transparent customs tariffs—which impose a predictable percentage tax at the border—non-tariff barriers operate behind the border through divergent 'technical product regulations, sanitary and phytosanitary (SPS) standards, licensing quotas, and mandatory local conformity-assessment procedures'. Trade economists estimate that the ad valorem tariff equivalent of non-tariff barriers frequently exceeds 15 % to 30 % in sectors such as pharmaceuticals, medical devices, agri-food, chemicals, and digital services.",
"While many technical and health standards serve legitimate public-policy objectives—such as safeguarding consumer safety, preventing agricultural disease transmission, and protecting environmental quality—governments frequently weaponise 'regulatory protectionism' by designing technical specifications tailored exclusively to domestic incumbents' manufacturing processes while refusing to recognise equivalent foreign safety certifications.",
"A pervasive operational burden arises from 'duplicative testing and local conformity-assessment mandates'. Even when an exported industrial turbine, telecom router, or pharmaceutical ingredient already meets stringent European CE or United States FDA safety benchmarks, importing jurisdictions routinely require foreign manufacturers to repeat exhaustive clinical trials, crash tests, or laboratory inspections inside domestic state-owned testing institutes—adding twelve to twenty-four months of delay and millions of euros in fixed market-entry overhead.",
"In recent years, non-tariff market-access barriers have expanded into 'sustainability, traceability, and digital governance requirements'. Regulations such as the European Union Deforestation Regulation (EUDR), mandatory battery carbon-footprint passports, and foreign data-localisation or source-code disclosure mandates impose formidable administrative compliance burdens that disproportionately exclude small and medium-sized exporters from developing economies lacking sophisticated digital auditing systems.",
