"texte": [
"INTERNATIONAL TRADE LAW & LOGISTICS CONTRACT ADVISORY NOTE — GLOBAL SUPPLY CHAIN & COMMERCIAL LAW PRACTICE (LONDON - ROTTERDAM - SINGAPORE). To: Export Sales, Procurement, and Logistics Management Teams, 'Euro-Machinery & Precision Systems plc'. Subject: Eliminating costly risk-transfer mismatches, customs clearance bottlenecks, and letter-of-credit discrepancies through rigorous application of ICC Incoterms 2020. Published and updated decennially by the International Chamber of Commerce (ICC), Incoterms rules define the exact contractual division of transport costs, export/import customs formalities, insurance obligations, and physical risk of loss or damage between buyer and seller in international merchandise trade.",
"Despite their universal adoption, commercial disputes frequently erupt because executives conflate two fundamentally distinct legal moments: the 'point of cost transfer' (who pays freight charges to a named destination) and the 'point of physical risk transfer' (when liability for accidental cargo loss or damage passes from seller to buyer). Under the widely used C-rules—such as CIF (Cost, Insurance and Freight) and CIP (Carriage and Insurance Paid To)—the seller pays main carriage expenses all the way to the destination port or terminal, yet physical risk transfers to the buyer much earlier, the instant goods are loaded aboard the vessel or handed over to the first carrier at origin.",
"A second pervasive operational error is the uncritical use of traditional maritime-only terms—FOB (Free On Board) and CIF—for 'containerised multimodal shipments'. Because containerised cargo is handed over to terminal operators at inland container yards days before crane loading onto a vessel, using FOB leaves the seller exposed to terminal damage risk after losing physical custody—prompting the ICC to recommend FCA (Free Carrier) and CIP for containerised transport.",
"At the opposite extremes of the Incoterms spectrum lie 'EXW (Ex Works)' and 'DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)', both of which create severe hidden compliance traps when applied carelessly across international borders. Under EXW, a foreign buyer is legally responsible for loading cargo at the seller's factory and clearing export customs—tasks a foreign entity often lacks legal registration to execute in the seller's country, frequently resulting in invalid VAT zero-rating audit penalties for the exporter.",
"Conversely, agreeing to 'DDP (Delivered Duty Paid)' obliges the exporter to clear import customs, obtain local product certifications, and pay import duties and local value-added taxes inside the buyer's foreign jurisdiction—exposing an unregistered foreign seller to tax permanent-establishment liabilities and indefinite customs seizure if local import licences are delayed.",
"Equally perilous is 'misalignment between Incoterms selection and documentary Letters of Credit (LCs)'. When a sales contract specifies EXW or FCA—where the buyer's nominated freight forwarder controls the international bill of lading—while payment depends on a bank Letter of Credit requiring the seller to present an on-board marine bill of lading, an unscrupulous buyer can instruct its forwarder to withhold the transport document, preventing the seller from collecting payment from the bank.",
"To prevent silent margin leakage and uninsured transit losses, leading export-import organisations enforce an 'Incoterms Governance Matrix'. Sales and procurement teams default to FCA (seller's premises or named carrier terminal) for containerised exports, specify CIP with Institute Cargo Clauses (A) all-risk insurance for high-value machinery, replace DDP with DAP (Delivered at Place) so the local buyer handles domestic import customs, and explicitly stipulate 'Incoterms 2020' alongside exact named geographical coordinates in every contract.",
"Ultimately, Incoterms are far more than shipping shorthand labels printed on commercial invoices; they are precision legal instruments governing cost, customs compliance, and casualty risk across borders. Mastering their exact mechanics protects commercial margins and prevents costly logistics disputes."
],
