"texte": [
"GLOBAL VALUE CHAIN ARCHITECTURE & INDUSTRIAL COMPETITIVENESS ESSAY — EUROPEAN INSTITUTE FOR INDUSTRIAL STRATEGY AND ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT (LONDON - BRUSSELS - SINGAPORE). Over the past thirty years, the fragmentation of production across borders into finely sliced 'Global Value Chains (GVCs)' transformed world trade—allowing developing economies to industrialise rapidly by specialising in specific assembly stages while enabling advanced economies to focus on upstream research and downstream branding.",
"Economists illustrate this distribution of value capture through Stan Shih's famous 'Smiling Curve': the highest profit margins and value-added sit at the two elevated ends of the chain—upstream basic R&D, proprietary component design, and intellectual property on the left, and downstream brand equity, software ecosystems, and after-market services on the right—while midstream physical assembly in the bottom trough captures the lowest commodity margins.",
"Today, however, three structural revolutions—artificial-intelligence automation, the green energy transition, and geoeconomic regionalisation—are radically reshaping both the geography and profit distribution of global value chains. As intelligent robotics and additive manufacturing automate routine assembly, low hourly labour wages are rapidly losing their historical power as a decisive source of industrial competitiveness.",
"Simultaneously, clean-energy industrial value chains—spanning solar photovoltaics, lithium-ion batteries, green hydrogen electrolysers, and electric vehicles—exhibit a markedly different value-capture geometry. In these capital- and material-intensive sectors, critical midstream chemical refining and advanced automated cell manufacturing command high strategic rents alongside upstream mineral processing, challenging the assumption that physical manufacturing inevitably yields low margins.",
"Confronted with this shifting industrial landscape, both corporations and governments are pursuing 'functional upgrading and twin green-digital transformation strategies'. Rather than competing on low-cost volume assembly, competitive enterprises upgrade along four trajectories: 'process upgrading' (deploying AI predictive quality control and zero-defect automation), 'product upgrading' (embedding smart sensors and recyclable circular materials), 'functional upgrading' (moving into proprietary design and recurring service contracts), and 'intersectoral upgrading' (applying core engineering capabilities to adjacent high-growth sectors such as aerospace or clean energy).",
