"texte": [
"AFRICAN SOVEREIGN DEBT & MACRO-FINANCIAL POLICY ESSAY — PAN-AFRICAN INSTITUTE FOR ECONOMIC GOVERNANCE AND DEVELOPMENT POLICY (ADDIS ABABA - DAKAR - ACCRA). Over the past two decades, structural development financing gaps—driven by rapid demographic expansion, urgent climate-adaptation needs, and vast infrastructure deficits—have led numerous African states to rely heavily on external capital inflows, spanning concessional multilateral loans, bilateral resource-backed infrastructure credits, and international sovereign Eurobond issuance.",
"While external borrowing enabled vital investments in power generation, ports, and telecommunications during the 2010s era of ultra-low global interest rates, over-reliance on foreign-currency debt has exposed African economies to severe 'external vulnerability and policy-autonomy erosion'. Economists term this structural fragility 'original sin': borrowing predominantly in US dollars or euros while collecting domestic tax revenues in local currencies.",
"When global monetary tightening by the United States Federal Reserve and European Central Bank triggered sharp appreciations of the US dollar alongside surging global yields, local-currency debt-service burdens across Sub-Saharan Africa escalated dramatically—pushing average external debt-service-to-revenue ratios above 25 % to 35 % and forcing several sovereigns into painful debt restructurings under the G20 Common Framework.",
"Equally concerning for economic sovereignty is the 'creditor-fragmentation deadlock' complicating modern African debt workouts. Unlike twentieth-century sovereign debt crises resolved relatively swiftly through Paris Club bilateral negotiations, contemporary African external debt is dispersed across private Eurobond asset managers, bilateral state development banks, and commodity-collateralised commercial lenders—creating protracted multi-year restructuring impasses that freeze fresh development investment.",
"Furthermore, heavy dependence on external conditional financing frequently narrows domestic democratic policy space. When debt-distressed states must negotiate emergency balance-of-payments programmes with international financial institutions or pledge future mineral and customs revenues as collateral for bilateral loans, national parliaments face rigid fiscal austerity mandates that curtail public spending on healthcare, education, and industrial diversification.",
"Reclaiming genuine African economic sovereignty requires shifting decisively from external debt dependency toward 'structural domestic resource mobilisation and local-currency capital markets'. Across Sub-Saharan Africa, average tax-to-GDP ratios remain near 15 % to 16 %—well below the 22 % to 25 % threshold required for self-sustaining state capacity—due to narrow tax bases, excessive multinational mining tax exemptions, and illicit financial outflows estimated at over 88 billion US dollars annually by UNCTAD.",
"Strengthening fiscal autonomy therefore demands modernising digital tax administration, renegotiating equitable mineral royalty and local-content regimes, curbing illicit transfer-pricing outflows, deepening domestic local-currency bond markets, and leveraging continental financial institutions—including the African Export-Import Bank (Afreximbank), the Africa Finance Corporation (AFC), and the proposed African Monetary Institute.",
"Ultimately, economic sovereignty in an interdependent world does not mean autarkic isolation from global capital markets. True African sovereignty lies in building deep domestic fiscal capacity, local-currency financing engines, and regional monetary institutions so that external capital serves national development priorities on equitable terms."
