"SUB-SAHARAN AFRICA INVESTMENT RISK & LEGAL PROTECTION ADVISORY NOTE — INTERNATIONAL PROJECT FINANCE & POLITICAL RISK PRACTICE (LONDON - JOHANNESBURG - NAIROBI). To: The Global Investment Committee of 'Euro-Energy & Telecoms Infrastructure Holdings plc'. Subject: Pricing and mitigating sovereign regulatory shifts, resource nationalism, currency-transfer restrictions, and electoral transition risks across Sub-Saharan African markets. Boasting the world's fastest-growing urban youth demographic, abundant renewable energy potential, and rapidly expanding digital adoption, Sub-Saharan Africa offers compelling long-term risk-adjusted returns—provided institutional investors distinguish genuine structural hazards from undifferentiated continental stereotypes.",
"Experienced infrastructure and private-equity investors recognise that outright state nationalisation of foreign assets has become rare in contemporary Sub-Saharan Africa; far more prevalent is 'creeping regulatory expropriation and fiscal-regime instability'. Once a foreign consortium sinks hundreds of millions of dollars into immobile mining, telecommunications, or power assets, host governments facing acute budget deficits frequently enact retroactive mining royalty hikes, windfall profit taxes, arbitrary telecom spectrum fees, or forced local-equity dilution mandates.",
