"texte": [
"AFRICAN INFRASTRUCTURE CAPITAL MOBILISATION BRIEFING — PAN-AFRICAN INFRASTRUCTURE INVESTMENT & DEVELOPMENT FINANCE FORUM (JOHANNESBURG - ABIDJAN - LONDON). Bridging Africa's transformative infrastructure needs—spanning cross-border railway logistics corridors, regional power pools, green hydrogen hubs, urban water treatment, and hyperscale data centres—requires mobilising upwards of 150 billion US dollars annually. With traditional sovereign tax revenues and concessional aid unable to fund more than a third of this requirement, unlocking large-scale private institutional capital has become the defining imperative of African economic development.",
"Paradoxically, global institutional investors—including pension funds, sovereign wealth funds, and insurance companies managing over 100 trillion US dollars worldwide—actively seek long-duration, inflation-linked infrastructure yields, yet allocate less than 1 % of their infrastructure portfolios to Africa. Development finance practitioners emphasize that this bottleneck stems not from a shortage of global capital, but from an 'acute bankable-project preparation deficit'.",
