    "texte": [
      "PUBLIC-SECTOR CORPORATE GOVERNANCE & SOE REFORM MEMORANDUM — PAN-AFRICAN PUBLIC ENTERPRISE GOVERNANCE AND FISCAL REFORM INSTITUTE (JOHANNESBURG - NAIROBI - ABIDJAN). Across Sub-Saharan Africa and major emerging economies, State-Owned Enterprises (SOEs)—dominating strategic sectors including power utilities, national airlines, port authorities, railway operators, development banks, and mining holdings—serve as vital instruments of public infrastructure delivery and industrial policy. Yet chronic governance dysfunctions frequently convert flagship public enterprises from engines of structural transformation into heavy drains on national treasuries.",
      "Economists attribute persistent SOE underperformance primarily to 'conflicting commercial and non-commercial mandates'. Simultaneously expected to operate profitably on commercial balance sheets while subsidising below-cost electricity tariffs, maintaining loss-making rural transport routes, and absorbing political patronage employment, public-enterprise executives lack unambiguous financial accountability when operating deficits accumulate.",
      "Compounding mandate ambiguity is the pathology of 'soft budget constraints and implicit sovereign bailout guarantees', famously theorised by economist János Kornai. Because state-owned utilities and national carriers know that governments rarely allow strategic public monopolies to enter liquidation, management teams face weakened incentives to control payroll bloat, curb procurement leakage, or enforce revenue collection from delinquent public-sector ministries.",
      "Equally corrosive is 'politicised board appointments and executive turnover volatility'. When board seats are distributed as political rewards rather than allocated through competency-based recruitment—and chief executives are replaced abruptly following every ministerial reshuffle—long-term capital planning collapses into short-term crisis management.",
      "In capital-intensive utility and transport sectors, chronic SOE losses trigger severe 'macroeconomic and fiscal contagion'. When state power or water utilities cannot fund grid maintenance or service state-guaranteed commercial debt from operating cash flows, recurring sovereign bailout transfers crowd out public spending on primary healthcare, technical education, and debt-service sustainability.",
      "Restoring operational excellence and financial self-sufficiency requires implementing the 'OECD Guidelines on Corporate Governance of State-Owned Enterprises' through four structural reforms. First, institutionalising 'centralised ownership entities' (such as sovereign holding companies in Singapore's Temasek or Morocco's Ithmar Capital model) that separate the state's role as professional shareholder from line ministries' sector-regulation duties.",
      "Second, codifying explicit 'Public Service Obligation (PSO) contracts'—requiring the national treasury to compensate SOEs transparently on-budget for any government-mandated below-cost social tariffs; third, enforcing merit-based, independent boards and IFRS audited public financial reporting; and fourth, pursuing 'minority public stock-exchange listings' (partial privatisation) that subject state enterprises to daily capital-market disclosure discipline without surrendering strategic sovereign control.",
      "Ultimately, state-owned enterprises need not be synonymous with bureaucratic inefficiency or fiscal bailouts. Governed with clear commercial mandates, transparent public-service accounting, and independent professional boards, strategic SOEs can deliver world-class public infrastructure while generating sustainable dividends for national development."
    ],
